rsion in `setup.py`, installation tools recognize your code as a formal, installable package that can be managed across different environments.\n3. **Leveraging Configuration Options:** Consider using configuration options like `pyproject.toml` alongside your `setup.py` script. These configuration files specify build systems and dependencies so that packaging tools know precisely how to compile and prepare your distribution archives.\n\nIf you attempted to install a package without a setup configuration file, management tools would have no information regarding the package name, version, or entry points, making installation impossible.\n\n## Deeper Dive\n\nDelving deeper into how setup scripts function, `setuptools` relies heavily on the metadata you provide to generate distribution records. When a user installs your package, Python records the metadata so it can track which version is installed and where its files reside. Getting these details right ensures that your package integrates seamlessly with Python's ecosystem management tools.\n\n## Common Pitfalls\n\nA common pitfall for beginners is misspelling configuration filenames—such as naming the file `setups.py` instead of `setup.py`—which causes packaging tools to ignore the file entirely. Another mistake is leaving out essential fields like the project name or version number, which will cause installation commands to error out. Always double-check your metadata declarations before proceeding to installation steps.\n\n## Summary\n\nIn this module, you learned how to create a project configuration file like `setup.py` or `pyproject.toml` to define basic package metadata including name, version number, and author information for installation tools. We explored how this setup allows installation tools to recognize your code as a formal package. Moving forward, the next module will guide you through writing clean modular code and performing local installations using `pip`.\n\n---\n\n# Module 4: Importing and Using