w appears on their screen even though the code executed successfully. Developers also sometimes mismatch the lengths of their `x` and `y` lists or arrays, which triggers dimension mismatch errors in Matplotlib. Finally, confusing the arguments of `subplot(m, n, p)` can lead to overlapping visual elements or plots failing to appear in the expected grid positions.\n\n## Summary\nIn this module, you learned how to use the Matplotlib library for data visualization in Python. You explored installation steps using package managers, imported plotting modules, and learned how to draw lines, bar graphs, and pie charts using built-in functions like `plot()`, `bar()`, and `grid()`. You also discovered how to organize multiple graphs using `subplot(m, n, p)` and how rendering works with `plt.show()`. These visualization skills build directly upon your knowledge of lists and arrays, empowering you to analyze and present data clearly as you progress toward more advanced programming concepts.'}, artifact_delta={}, transfer_to_agent=None, escalate=None, requested_auth_configs={}, requested_tool_confirmations={}, compaction=None, end_of_agent=None, agent_state=None, rewind_before_invocation_id=None, route=None, render_ui_widgets=None, set_model_response=None) output=None node_info=NodeInfo(path='', output_for=None, message_as_output=None) long_running_tool_ids=None branch=None isolation_scope=None id='2b14840a-6f57-4b24-8bb0-463cf3cf54c3' timestamp=1789454785.9405038
