DEBUG    | 2026-09-15 15:15:08 | course_creator:run_adk_curriculum_for_document:170 | Event from ResearchAgent: model_version='gemini-3.5-flash-lite' content=Content(
  parts=[
    Part(
      text="""{
    "Linear Algebra Foundations": "Notes based on Michael U. Gutmann's 'Pen & Paper Exercises in Machine Learning': Covers vector and matrix operations, eigenvalue decomposition, trace, determinants, symmetric matrices, the power method, and Gram-Schmidt orthogonalisation. Specifically, Gram-Schmidt orthogonalisation constructs an orthogonal basis from a set of linearly independent vectors by iteratively subtracting projections onto previously computed orthogonal vectors. If a vector is a linear combination of previous ones, the resulting orthogonal vector is zero.",
    "Optimization Methods": "Notes based on Gutmann's text: Focuses on unconstrained optimization and vector/matrix calculus. Key topics include computing gradients of vector-valued and matrix-valued functions, Newton's method for root-finding or optimization, gradients of log-determinants, and deriving descent directions for matrix-valued functions.",
    "Directed Graphical Models": "Notes based on Gutmann's text: Covers Bayesian networks and probabilistic dependency structures. Concepts include directed graph definitions, canonical connections, ordered and local Markov properties, d-separation, Hidden Markov Models (HMMs), alternative characterisations of independencies, and specific case studies such as the chest clinic example.",
    "Undirected Graphical Models": "Notes based on Gutmann's text: Covers Markov random fields and Gibbs distributions. Key themes include visualising and analysing Gibbs distributions via undirected graphs, factorisation properties, conditional independence, Markov blankets, undirected models with pairwise potentials, Restricted Boltzmann Machines (RBMs), and connections to Hidden Markov Models.",
    "Factor Graphs and Message Passing": "Notes based on Gutmann's text: Covers the representation of joint distributions using factor graphs and exact inference algorithms. Topics include converting directed and undirected graphical models into factor graphs, sum-product message passing, max-sum message passing for finding optimal configurations, and the choice of variable elimination orders.",
    "Model-Based Learning and Inference": "Notes based on Gutmann's text: Covers parameter estimation, probabilistic inference, sampling, and variational methods. Topics include Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) for Gaussians and discrete probability tables in directed models, Bayesian inference (including conjugate models like the Bernoulli and Gaussian models), factor analysis, independent component analysis (ICA), score matching, Monte Carlo integration, importance sampling, inverse transform sampling, rejection sampling, Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) inference, and Mean Field Variational Inference."
}"""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
