{
    "Linear Algebra Foundations and Decompositions": "The document covers linear algebra exercises from Michael U. Gutmann's 'Pen & Paper Exercises in Machine Learning'. Specific topics detailed include Gram-Schmidt orthogonalisation (showing orthogonality of computed vectors $u_1$ and $u_2$, handling scalar multiples that produce zero vectors, and expressing linear combinations of original vectors in terms of orthogonal vectors), linear transforms, eigenvalue decomposition, trace, determinants and eigenvalues, eigenvalue decomposition for symmetric matrices, and the power method.",
    "Matrix Optimization and Gradients": "The optimization section of the document covers gradients of vector-valued functions, Newton's method, gradients of matrix-valued functions, gradients of the log-determinant, and descent directions for matrix-valued functions.",
    "Directed and Undirected Graphical Models": "The document covers directed graphical models (directed graph concepts, canonical connections, ordered and local Markov properties, d-separation, chest clinic examples, hidden Markov models, and alternative/extended characterisations of independencies) and undirected graphical models (visualising and analysing Gibbs distributions via undirected graphs, factorisation, independencies, Markov blankets, pairwise potentials, restricted Boltzmann machines, and hidden Markov models with change of measure). It also discusses the expressive power of graphical models, including I-equivalence, minimal I-maps, moralisation (converting DAGs to undirected minimal I-maps), triangulation, and limits of directed vs undirected graphical models.",
    "Factor Graphs and Inference Methods": "The document covers factor graphs and message passing algorithms (conversion to factor graphs, sum-product message passing, max-sum message passing, and choice of elimination order). It also addresses inference for hidden Markov models, including predictive distributions, the Viterbi algorithm, forward filtering backward sampling, prediction exercises, change of measure, and Kalman filtering.",
    "Model-Based Learning, Sampling, and Variational Inference": "The document covers model-based learning (maximum likelihood estimation for Gaussians, posterior of the mean of a Gaussian with known variance, MLE and Bayesian inference for probability tables in fully observed directed graphical models of binary variables, cancer-asbestos-smoking examples, parameter learning, factor analysis, independent component analysis, score matching for the exponential family, and parameter estimation for unnormalised models). It also explores sampling and Monte Carlo integration (importance sampling, inverse transform sampling, exponential and Laplace distributions, rejection sampling, sampling from restricted Boltzmann machines, basic Markov chain Monte Carlo inference, Bayesian Poisson regression, and mixing/convergence of Metropolis-Hastings MCMC), as well as variational inference (mean field variational inference and variational posterior approximations)."
}
```"""
