      text="""**Definition**: The gradient of a function $J$ mapping a column vector $w \in \mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}$ is defined as a column vector of its partial derivatives with respect to each element of $w$.

**Key points**:
- The gradient $\nabla J(w)$ contains the partial derivatives $\partial J(w) / \partial w_i$ for each $i$-th element of $w$ in the standard basis.
- It can alternatively be defined via a first-order or affine approximation using an arbitrary perturbation $\epsilon h$.
- The derivative $\nabla J$ acts as a linear transformation mapping vectors $h \in \mathbb{R}^n$ to $\mathbb{R}$.
- The perturbation expansion is expressed as $J(w + \epsilon h) = J(w) + \epsilon (\nabla J(w))^\top h + O(\epsilon^2)$."""
    ),
  ],
  role='model'
